@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Support-Vector Networks — Max-Margin Classification
' Paper: Support-vector networks
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00994018
' Level: 1

component "Training pairs\nx_i with label y_i" as X <<app>>
component "Kernel map φ\nimplicit via K(x_i, x_j)" as PHI <<host>>
component "Quadratic program\nmaximize the margin" as QP <<app>>
database  "Support vectors\nnon-zero multipliers α_i" as SV <<db>>
component "Slack ξ_i, penalty C\nsoft margin" as SOFT <<async>>
component "Decision function\nsign of Σ α_i y_i K(x_i, x) + b" as DEC <<app>>

X ARROW_OPTIONAL PHI : lift to higher dimension
X ARROW_MAIN QP : separable case
PHI ARROW_MAIN QP : only inner products needed
SOFT ARROW_MAIN QP : allow violations\nwhen classes overlap
QP ARROW_MAIN SV : optimum is sparse
SV ARROW_MAIN DEC : prediction touches\nonly the support vectors

note bottom of QP
  The separating hyperplane is chosen to **maximize
  the geometric margin** (width 2/||w||), not merely
  to separate — that choice is what bounds
  generalization rather than training error.
  The //kernel trick// is the second idea: K supplies
  inner products in φ's space, so φ is never
  computed and may be infinite-dimensional.
  C trades margin width against violations.
  ----
  **Not fitted by gradient descent.** The kernelized
  dual is a //constrained// quadratic program, solved by
  QP — classically SMO — not by SGD. Subgradient
  methods (Pegasos) apply to the linear primal only.
  Contrast ""../optimization/gradient-descent.puml"".
end note
@enduml
